adaptor — provenance note
=========================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `bip340`/
`musig2` modules, and `tlsresume`/`dtls`/`falcon`/`slhdsa` — self-contained
crypto modules not folded into the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

adaptor — clean-room implementation of Schnorr adaptor signatures (a.k.a.
"one-time verifiably encrypted signatures", the scriptless-scripts
construction) over BIP340 Schnorr on secp256k1. Unlike `bip340`/`musig2`,
NO BIP or other formal specification numbers this scheme — there is no
`bitcoin/bips` document, no official test-vector file, and no single
canonical "the spec" to cite the way BIP340/BIP327 are cited elsewhere in
this repository. The construction implemented here (PreSign/PreVerify/
Adapt/Extract, and — critically — the `needs_negation` parity bookkeeping
that BIP340's even-y convention forces on any scheme that offsets a nonce
by an externally-chosen point) was assembled from, and cross-validated
against, the following public sources:

1. **secp256kfun / schnorr_fun** (`LLFourn/secp256kfun`, 0BSD license —
   https://github.com/LLFourn/secp256kfun,
   `schnorr_fun/src/adaptor/mod.rs` +
   `schnorr_fun/src/adaptor/encrypted_signature.rs`, fetched 2026-07-12).
   This is a DESIGN REFERENCE, not ported source (CONVENTIONS.md §5): its
   Rust `encrypted_sign`/`verify_encrypted_signature`/`decrypt_signature`/
   `recover_decryption_key` functions were read to confirm the EXACT
   parity/negation handling (the `needs_negation` flag: how it is derived
   during signing, why it must be transmitted explicitly rather than
   recomputed by a verifier, and how it flips the sign of the nonce scalar
   during signing versus the sign of the adaptor secret during
   decrypt/recover) — no source lines were copied; this module's Zig code,
   API shape (`preSign`/`preVerify`/`adapt`/`extract`, distinct from
   secp256kfun's `encrypted_sign`/`verify_encrypted_signature`/
   `decrypt_signature`/`recover_decryption_key` naming), types
   (`AdaptorPoint`, `PreSignature` — distinct from secp256kfun's
   `Point`/`EncryptedSignature`), wire serialization (this module's own
   65-byte `r || s_prime || needs_negation-byte` layout), and domain tags
   (`adaptor/aux`, `adaptor/nonce` — project-local names; secp256kfun uses
   a different nonce-derivation macro entirely) are independent designs
   built on `bip340`. 0BSD is public-domain-equivalent (no attribution
   condition), cited here purely as a design-reference courtesy per
   CONVENTIONS.md §5, not because any license term requires it.
2. **Bitcoin Optech, "Adaptor signatures" topic page**
   (https://bitcoinops.org/en/topics/adaptor-signatures/) — general
   conceptual confirmation of the PreSign/PreVerify/Adapt/Extract
   four-algorithm shape and the `s = r + t + H(R+T‖P‖m)*p` /
   `s' = s - t` relationship (i.e. the pre-signature omits the adaptor
   secret; `Adapt` adds it back).
3. **"Schnorr Adaptor Signature" (muens.io / Cyphertalk)**
   (https://muens.io/schnorr-adaptor-signature/) — general conceptual
   confirmation of the PreSign/PreVerify/Adapt/Extract naming and the
   `R + T` adapted-nonce idea (does not itself address BIP340's even-y
   parity requirement — that detail came from source (1) above).
4. **BIP340** (`bitcoin/bips`, public spec — see `../bip340/NOTICE`) — the
   base signature scheme this module extends; the even-y `lift_x`
   convention that FORCES the `needs_negation` bookkeeping in the first
   place is BIP340's, not this scheme's own invention.

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.
`std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1` / `std.crypto.hash.sha2.Sha256` (MIT, part of
Zig itself) and the sibling `bip340` module (this repository, MIT — see
`../bip340/NOTICE`) are used via their public APIs; no new curve or hash
primitive is implemented here.

Self-authored reference vectors
--------------------------------

`kat_vectors.zig`'s six vectors were computed independently in Python (a
from-scratch secp256k1 field/point implementation written for this pass —
not libsecp256k1, not `bitcoin-core/secp256k1`, not secp256kfun's Rust
code, and not any Zig code from this module) by directly transcribing the
9-step `preSign` construction documented in `root.zig`'s doc comments, then
cross-checked three independent ways (see `kat_vectors.zig`'s own doc
comment for the three checks) before being embedded verbatim. This Python
reference implementation was written for vector generation ONLY; it is not
part of the repository and is not itself a design reference for the Zig
code (the Zig `root.zig` doc comments are the authoritative construction
description, written first).

**Status: scaffold.** Wire codecs (`AdaptorPoint`, `PreSignature`) are
implemented and tested for real. The four crypto cores (`preSign`,
`preVerify`, `adapt`, `extract`) are `@panic("TODO(fable): ...")` stubs
with their final signatures fixed and their exact construction fully
specified in `root.zig`'s doc comments and `SPEC.md`; `kat_vectors.zig` /
`kat_test.zig` are ready to validate a crypto-core implementation
byte-exactly the moment one lands. See `SPEC.md`'s "TODO(fable)" section.
